rphisms.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":283.8811792965418,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":163.003,"r":249.70089043000004,"t":293.4149153},"charspan":[0,17],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/40","text":"are isomorphisms."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","enumerated":true,"label":"list_item","marker":"(4)","orig":"(4) The spectral sequences in the previous diagram degenerate, and the filtrations on de Rham cohomology are n -opposed, i.e. b p,q k = 0 unless k = p + q .","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":235.17114928971534,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":143.52499999999998,"r":485.32902393,"t":280.4629153},"charspan":[0,156],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/41","text":"The spectral sequences in the previous diagram degenerate, and the filtrations on de Rham cohomology are n -opposed, i.e. b p,q k = 0 unless k = p + q ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Moreover, for a compact K¨ ahler manifold X , the bicomplex A X satisfies these conditions.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":216.03117929654172,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.672,"r":485.32793999,"t":238.51691529999994},"charspan":[0,91],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/42","text":"Moreover, for a compact K¨ ahler manifold X , the bicomplex A X satisfies these conditions."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"A complex manifold X for which A X satisfies the above conditions is called a ∂ ¯ ∂ -manifold. Compact K¨ ahler manifolds are ∂ ¯ ∂ -manifolds, but the converse it not true. A broader class is for example given by those manifolds bimeromorphic to compact K¨ ahler manifolds, i.e. Fujiki's class C , but also these do not exhaust all ∂ ¯ ∂ -manifolds, see e.g. [Fri19], [Li24], [KS23a].","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":143.85517929654168,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.67200000000003,"r":485.3297657899996,"t":205.19391529999984},"charspan":[0,385],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/43","text":"A complex manifold X for which A X satisfies the above conditions is called a ∂ ¯ ∂ -manifold. Compact K¨ ahler manifolds are ∂ ¯ ∂ -manifolds, but the converse it not true. A broader class is for example given by those manifolds bimeromorphic to compact K¨ ahler manifolds, i.e. Fujiki's class C , but also these do not exhaust all ∂ ¯ ∂ -manifolds, see e.g. [Fri19], [Li24], [KS23a]."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"One readily checks that all cohomologies introduced above are compatible with direct sums, vanish on squares and are one-dimensional on dots. Thus, as soon as a cohomology allows to reconstruct the information on the position of the dots, e.g. Dolbeault cohomology, or de Rham cohomology","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":92.04917929654175,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":126.67200000000001,"r":485.3315716099996,"t":140.4369152999999},"charspan":[0,287],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/44","text":"One readily checks that all cohomologies introduced above are compatible with direct sums, vanish on squares and are one-dimensional on dots. Thus, as soon as a cohomology allows to reconstruct the information on the position of the dots, e.g. Dolbeault cohomology, or de Rham cohomology"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":295.4411492897154,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":305.01599999999996,"r":309.86291313,"t":314.8299153},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/45","text":"("},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":")","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":295.4411492897154,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":318.046,"r":322.89291313,"t":314.8299153},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/46","text":")"}],"version":"1.10.0"}